  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 69,
   "id": "a15fddf8",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Count summary values and station count using the manuscript station definition\n",
    "all_merged = pd.read_parquet(\"Final/merged_all_final.parquet\")\n",
    "\n",
    "records = len(all_merged)\n",
    "taxa = all_merged['aphiaID'].nunique()\n",
    "datasets = all_merged['datasetKey'].nunique()\n",
    "start_year = all_merged['year'].min()\n",
    "end_year = all_merged['year'].max()\n",
    "species_taxa = all_merged.loc[\n",
    "    all_merged['taxonRank'].astype('string').str.strip().eq('Species'),\n",
    "    'acceptedAphiaID'\n",
    "].nunique()\n",
    "gear_percentage = (all_merged['gear'].notna().sum() / records) * 100\n",
    "biomass_percentage = (all_merged['biomass'].notna().sum() / records) * 100\n",
    "\n",
    "# Station definition: datasetKey + rounded lat/lon (5 decimals) + rounded year; keep missing year\n",
    "station_cols_required = ['datasetKey', 'decimalLatitudeStart', 'decimalLongitudeStart']\n",
    "station_key = ['datasetKey', 'decimalLatitudeStart', 'decimalLongitudeStart', 'year', 'startDayOfYear']\n",
    "\n",
    "stations_df = all_merged[station_key].copy()\n",
    "stations_df['decimalLatitudeStart'] = pd.to_numeric(stations_df['decimalLatitudeStart'], errors='coerce').round(5)\n",
    "stations_df['decimalLongitudeStart'] = pd.to_numeric(stations_df['decimalLongitudeStart'], errors='coerce').round(5)\n",
    "stations_df['year'] = pd.to_numeric(stations_df['year'], errors='coerce').round().astype('Int64')\n",
    "stations_df['startDayOfYear'] = pd.to_numeric(stations_df['startDayOfYear'], errors='coerce').round().astype('Int64')\n",
    "stations_df = stations_df.dropna(subset=station_cols_required)\n",
    "\n",
    "stations = stations_df.drop_duplicates(station_key).shape[0]\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "375a2c9b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "*Abstract*"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "id": "ad5b5fef",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/markdown": [
       "As the Arctic Ocean undergoes rapid environmental change and is subjected to increasing anthropogenic pressures, there is a growing need for comprehensive baseline data on its biodiversity. Although sampling efforts have been considerable over the past decades, the existing biodiversity records are often fragmented and scattered across multiple databases. We present BENTH-MARC, an open pan-Arctic database with BENTHos in the Marine ARCtic. BENTH-MARC contains 3,760,265 georeferenced zoobenthic taxon records of 8,442 species and 3,450 taxa at other taxonomic levels from 826 datasets and 223,349 stations. Data span across the entire pan-Arctic realm and integrate regional and global biodiversity databases as well as previously unpublished datasets. The records in BENTH-MARC come from two and a half centuries of sampling, from 1773-2026, and are based on morphological identification. By providing harmonized and accessible data, BENTH-MARC can support the assessment of biological consequences of ongoing environmental change, aid in the identification of biodiversity hotspots and evaluation of conservation targets. The database can also be used to guide future sampling efforts."
      ],
      "text/plain": [
       "<IPython.core.display.Markdown object>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "display(Markdown(\n",
    "    f\"As the Arctic Ocean undergoes rapid environmental change and is subjected to increasing anthropogenic pressures, there is a growing need for comprehensive baseline data on its biodiversity. Although sampling efforts have been considerable over \"\n",
    "    f\"the past decades, the existing biodiversity records are often fragmented and scattered across multiple databases. We present BENTH-MARC, an open pan-Arctic database with BENTHos in the Marine ARCtic. BENTH-MARC contains \"\n",
    "    f\"{records:,.0f} georeferenced zoobenthic taxon records of {species_taxa:,.0f} species and {taxa - species_taxa:,.0f} taxa at other taxonomic levels from {datasets} datasets and {stations:,.0f} stations. Data span across the \"\n",
    "    f\"entire pan-Arctic realm and integrate regional and global biodiversity databases as well as previously unpublished datasets. \"\n",
    "    f\"The records in BENTH-MARC come from two and a half centuries of sampling, from {start_year:.0f}-{end_year:.0f}, and are based on morphological identification. \"\n",
    "    f\"By providing harmonized and accessible data, BENTH-MARC can support the assessment of biological consequences of ongoing environmental change, aid in the identification of biodiversity hotspots and evaluation of conservation targets. \"\n",
    "    f\"The database can also be used to guide future sampling efforts.\"\n",
    "))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8776c2d1-722b-4e14-8b78-4b3f50f45bd4",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Background\n",
    "\n",
    "The Arctic Ocean is experiencing rapid environmental changes and an increase in anthropogenic pressures [@overland2019; @bidgood2024], which is in part linked to better accessibility as the sea ice withdraws [@pame2025]. The demand on Arctic resources is expected to increase in the future, with growing interests in, among others, deep-sea mining, oil and gas extraction, shipping and tourism in the region [@bidgood2024]. Currently, there are knowledge gaps on how large-scale biodiversity patterns will respond to these rapid environmental changes and elevated anthropogenic pressures [@brandt2023]. Sampling efforts in the Arctic Ocean have been considerable over the past decades [@ramirezllodra2024], and data accessibility has increased due to improved storage options and the growing prevalence of mandatory data publication [@michner2015]. Nevertheless, existing biodiversity records are often insufficient, fragmented, and scattered across multiple databases [@caff2017]. This creates considerable uncertainties and poses challenges for the sustainable management of the Arctic Ocean.\n",
    "\n",
    "Benthic communities play a vital role in Arctic marine food webs, nutrient recycling, and the carbon cycle [@kedra2015], while also harboring the majority of marine biodiversity [@sirenko2001]. Furthermore, benthic organisms are widely regarded as key indicators of ecosystem health due to the strong coupling between environmental drivers and species composition and distribution patterns [@kraan2010; @kraan2015], as well as their comparatively long lifespans. As a result, studies investigating the impacts of environmentally hazardous activities, such as oil and gas extraction [@mauffrey2021; @chen2024] or infrastructure development [@leng2024], often rely on benthic communities in impact assessments. Understanding the current baseline of benthic biodiversity is therefore critical — not only for identifying present biodiversity hotspots but also for predicting the future state of the Arctic Ocean and for supporting sustainable development of environmentally hazardous activities. However, sampling zoobenthos in the Arctic Ocean presents significant challenges, including high costs, logistical difficulties, and limited accessibility. These challenges are particularly pronounced in deep-water areas, where research efforts remain scarce. Most studies to date have concentrated on sea ice, surface waters, and the water column, leaving substantial gaps in our understanding of deep benthic environments [@bluhm2011; @ramirezllodra2024]. To ensure that future sampling efforts target the areas of greatest need, it is essential to create an overview of the availability, structure, and coverage of existing data and metadata. Additionally, facilitating reuse of already existing data helps maximize the value of each sampling campaign. \n",
    "\n",
    "Previous large-scale compilations of Arctic benthic biodiversity include the works of Bluhm *et al.* [-@bluhm2011] and Ramirez Llodra *et al.* [-@ramirezllodra2024] on the Arctic basins, a taxon compilation by Sirenko *et al.* [-@sirenko2001] on the Russian Arctic Seas, an inventory of Arctic shelf seas by Piepenburg *et al.* [-@piepenburg2011], and a pan-Arctic overview of both shelves and basins by Piepenburg *et al.* [-@piepenburg2024]. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 70,
   "id": "f3a53f81",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/markdown": [
       "In a time of global change and competing sea-use interests, large-scale analyses at high taxonomic resolution are key for informing effective science-based decision-making. Such analyses can be facilitated and improved by access to harmonized biodiversity and biomass data. Here, we present BENTH-MARC, an open pan-Arctic database with a focus on BENTHos in the Marine ARCtic. BENTH-MARC includes 3,760,265 zoobenthic records from the Global Biodiversity Information Facility (GBIF) [@gbif2026], Ocean Biodiversity Information System (OBIS) [@obis2026], CRITTERBASE  [@teschke2022], Observatoire Global du Saint-Laurent (OGSL) [@ogsl2026] and previously unpublished datasets from Aarhus University. Each record represents an observation of a certain taxon at a specific location and time. This means that for example 200 individuals of the same taxon, location and date count as a single record. The records span over the Arctic Large Marine Ecosystems [@pame2013] and date back to as early as 1773. The records have been cleaned and harmonized and are now available for download in different formats. By providing harmonized and accessible data, BENTH-MARC can support the assessment of biological consequences of ongoing environmental change and can be used to guide future sampling efforts. "
      ],
      "text/plain": [
       "<IPython.core.display.Markdown object>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "display(Markdown(\n",
    "    f\"In a time of global change and competing sea-use interests, large-scale analyses at high taxonomic resolution are key for informing effective science-based decision-making. Such analyses can \"\n",
    "    f\"be facilitated and improved by access to harmonized biodiversity and biomass data. Here, we present BENTH-MARC, an open pan-Arctic database with a focus on BENTHos in the Marine ARCtic. BENTH-MARC \"\n",
    "    f\"includes {records:,.0f} zoobenthic records from the Global Biodiversity Information Facility (GBIF) [@gbif2026], Ocean Biodiversity Information System (OBIS) [@obis2026], CRITTERBASE  [@teschke2022], Observatoire \"\n",
    "    f\"Global du Saint-Laurent (OGSL) [@ogsl2026] and previously unpublished datasets from Aarhus University. Each record represents an observation of a certain taxon at a specific location and time. This means that for example 200 individuals of the \"\n",
    "    f\"same taxon, location and date count as a single record. The records span over the Arctic Large Marine Ecosystems [@pame2013] \"\n",
    "    f\"and date back to as early as {start_year:.0f}. The records have been cleaned and harmonized and are now available for download in different formats. By providing harmonized and accessible data, \"\n",
    "    f\"BENTH-MARC can support the assessment of biological consequences of ongoing environmental change and can be used to guide future sampling efforts. \"\n",
    "))"
   ]
