   "source": [
    "## Data sources"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "03ed3a31-ba92-4916-b17e-f8969e8d3c3a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "BENTH-MARC includes publicly available, and previously unpublished, georeferenced and morphologically identified metazoan datasets of meio-, macro- and megabenthos in the Arctic LMEs (@fig-map_stations). Only invertebrates were included, more specifically from the following phyla: Annelida, Mollusca, Arthropoda, Echinodermata, Cnidaria, Porifera, Sipuncula, Bryozoa, Chordata, Brachiopoda, Nematoda, Nemertea, Tardigrada, Platyhelminthes, Hemichordata, Kinorhyncha, Priapulida, Loricifera, Phoronida and Entoprocta. Data were extracted from GBIF and OBIS on 2026-05-05. The records were downloaded using the R packages rGBIF  [@rgbif2026] and robis [@robis2022], which offer programmatic access to the application programming interface (API) of GBIF and OBIS. To access associated metadata and information, complementary downloads targeted the related Extended Measurement or Fact (eMoF) extensions. The citations of the OBIS and GBIF derived datasets can be found at (*link needs to be created*) and (*link needs to be created*). Data from CRITTERBASE were downloaded through Alfred Wegener's Institute's dedicated web portal on 2026-02-06. Data from OGSL and Aarhus University were retrieved through personal communication [@archambault2026; @sejr2025]. \n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d2f020c4",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Data filtering"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 9,
   "id": "f3b33270",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/markdown": [
       "The main value of BENTH-MARC lies in the compilation of available Arctic benthic faunal data in one place and the rigorous data filtering and harmonization process. The total number of records from the study area was at the time of download 56,102,941, which has been reduced to 3,760,265. This corresponds to a reduction by 93.3% of the original records. "
      ],
      "text/plain": [
       "<IPython.core.display.Markdown object>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "display(Markdown(\n",
    "    f\"The main value of BENTH-MARC lies in the compilation of available Arctic benthic faunal data in one place and the rigorous data filtering and harmonization process. \"\n",
    "    f\"The total number of records from the study area was at the time of download \"\n",
    "    f\"56,102,941, which has been reduced to {records:,.0f}. This corresponds to a reduction by {(1 - records / 56102941) * 100:.1f}% of the original records. \"\n",
    "   ))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "19dc2235-7c18-4291-b39d-cf48ae388304",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Extensive filtering has been necessary as the downloads of all records in the abovementioned phyla from GBIF and OBIS led to an inclusion of a considerable number of pelagic, terrestrial, limnic and extinct taxa. These taxa were at a first stage filtered out using a comprehensive taxonomic filter at the levels of class, order, family, genus and species (Table S1, Supplementary material). GBIF and OBIS use different taxonomic backbones, and whereas GBIF's identifier is based on 105 different sources such as Catalogue of Life Checklist and WoRMS [@gbifbackbone], OBIS, CRITTERBASE and OGSL use Aphia ID as identifier and thus only rely on WoRMS. The taxa in previously unpublished datasets from Aarhus University had not been mapped to an identifier and Aphia IDs were extracted as part of preparing the datasets for integration in BENTH-MARC. The taxonomic filter used to produce BENTH-MARC has been tailored to work with both GBIF's identifier and Aphia ID. \n",
    "\n",
    "When the taxonomic filters had been applied, the remaining taxa were matched to functional group as assigned by the World Registry of Marine Species (WoRMS) [@wormsen], which provides information about whether the taxa can be considered benthic or pelagic. The taxa were also matched to their respective environment through WoRMS, which classifies all taxa as marine, brackish, fresh or terrestrial [@wormsen]. Furthermore, information about the taxon's status as extant or extinct was retrieved through WoRMS. These steps enabled a further elimination of any irrelevant taxa that remained after the first taxonomic filter. WoRMS has 80% coverage in terms of functional group [@wormsfu] and 100% coverage in terms of environment for accepted, extant, marine species [@wormsen]. Whenever a taxon lacked functional groups information, it was inferred from a higher taxonomic level. Only records belonging to extant marine and brackish benthic taxa were kept for further processing. All information about functional groups, environment and status as extant or extinct was accessed through the R package worrms [@worrms]. \n",
    "\n",
    "All records belonging to datasets with molecular-, telemetry- or acoustic-based identification or with a main focus on zooplankton, fish or sympagic taxa were removed. Citizen-science datasets without expert-level quality control were deleted. Records sampled with typically pelagic gear such as plankton net and continuous plankton recorder were excluded, and fossil specimens were filtered out. Our filters also targeted the georeferencing quality, removing all records with coordinate uncertainty of >0.01 degrees or >10,000 meters. Records with the known default uncertainty values 301, 3036, 999, and 9999 were removed, as they typically have a higher uncertainty than stated by the given value. This was done in accordance with GBIF recommendations [@gbif2021]. All records denoting absence were excluded. \n",
    "\n",
    "Coastline representations in polar regions exhibit larger errors due to factors such as intensified climate‑driven coastal instability [@irrgang2022] and ambiguity between sea ice and open water in satellite imagery [@nylen2025]. This limits the applicability of mapped coastlines for filtering out records that have mistakes in their coordinates and where the stations appear on land. At the same time, the prevalence of fjord-rich landscapes with drastic changes in elevation mean that the available bathymetry layers do not offer high enough spatial resolution to be suitable as a basis for filtering. We therefore manually removed stations that are clearly on land instead of using a programmatic approach, but did not remove ambiguous cases close to the coastlines. \n",
    "\n",
    "CRITTERBASE, GBIF and OBIS have considerable data overlap, but the databases often hold varying degrees of associated information such as abundance, biomass and gear type for the same records. The same is also true for within-database duplicates, as two datasets in GBIF or OBIS can contain varying degrees of associated information for the same record. To preserve as much of the desired information as possible, selected columns were keyed in from the eMoF files prior to the deduplication process. The deduplication process removed records with less associated information, preferentially preserving records that included (in decreasing order of priority) area or volume sampled, trawling distance, biomass, gear type, depth, and number of individuals. The deduplication itself was carried out in two steps. The first round targeted dataset duplicates and only kept one version of each dataset, generally preserving the version with the largest number of records and most associated information. When the degree of associated information was significantly higher in a dataset version with a slightly lower number of records, manual edits ensured that this version was preserved. The second round of deduplication targeted stations and was based on the entire list of taxa associated with each station. A station was defined as each unique combination of coordinates, year and day of the year. Du to incomplete coverage in the year and day of the year columns, some stations were only based on unique combinations of coordinates. Whenever two stations shared the exact same taxon list, the station with less associated information in the abovementioned eMoF columns was removed. In total, 320 955 records were considered duplicates and were filtered out. \n",
    "    \n",
    "## Data compilation \n",
    "We carried out a data harmonization process to enable a merge between records from OBIS, GBIF, CRITTERBASE and OGSL. This included creating a controlled glossary and collecting associated information with labels such as samplingProtocol, Gear and Sampling device in one column. As a general rule, Darwin Core terms have been preferred. \n",
    "\n",
    "As previously mentioned, GBIF uses its own taxonomic backbone and therefore does not include Aphia IDs. Instead, Aphia IDs were retrieved for all taxa in the GBIF subset through the r package worrms [@worrms], using the first match and manually processing taxa where no match could be found. As a rule of thumb, the Aphia ID of a higher taxonomic level was manually assigned to taxa when no match could be found at the given level. OBIS, CRITTERBASE and OGSL datasets already had AphiaIDs assigned to each taxon. To ensure that the records were updated against the latest taxonomic information available on WoRMS, all records from all sources were stripped of all taxonomic information, including scientific name, rank, classification and taxon status, apart from AphiaID. The taxonomic information was then harvested anew from WoRMS using worrms [@worrms], to ensure that all taxonomic information is up-to-date and has been retrieved on the same date for all taxa. Also here, the Aphia ID of a higher taxonomic level was manually assigned to taxa when no match could be found at the given level. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "59f0e137-f552-4986-b55c-e6b5cb1c42aa",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "# Data records\n",
    "The data is available as two separate files; one Parquet file with all occurrence records in a long format, and one CSV file with dataset-level metadata. The column datasetKey links these two files. Parquet is a column-oriented data storage format that facilitates efficient data processing and retrieval of big data. Unlike row-based formats like CSV, Parquet organizes data by columns, which allows for better compression and faster analytical queries of big datasets [@parquet2022]. Although there are no strict upper limits to how much data you can store in a CSV file, analyzing large CSV files requires significantly more processing power than Parquet files. To accommodate users who cannot access the data in Parquet format, the occurrence records are also available as one CSV file per LME. \n",
    "\n",
    "All files are available on Zenodo (insert URL). "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a113035f-0a1d-4ddb-a18e-22b70051f5c9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Data overview"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 12,
   "id": "05ce8312",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Count summary values and station count using the manuscript station definition\n",
    "all_merged = pd.read_parquet(\"Final/merged_all_final.parquet\")\n",
    "\n",
    "records = len(all_merged)\n",
    "taxa = all_merged['aphiaID'].nunique()\n",
    "datasets = all_merged['datasetKey'].nunique()\n",
    "start_year = all_merged['year'].min()\n",
    "end_year = all_merged['year'].max()\n",
    "species_taxa = all_merged.loc[\n",
    "    all_merged['taxonRank'].astype('string').str.strip().eq('Species'),\n",
    "    'acceptedAphiaID'\n",
    "].nunique()\n",
    "gear_percentage = (all_merged['gear'].notna().sum() / records) * 100\n",
    "biomass_percentage = (all_merged['biomass'].notna().sum() / records) * 100\n",
    "\n",
    "# Station definition: datasetKey + rounded lat/lon (5 decimals) + rounded year; keep missing year\n",
    "station_cols_required = ['datasetKey', 'decimalLatitudeStart', 'decimalLongitudeStart']\n",
    "station_key = ['datasetKey', 'decimalLatitudeStart', 'decimalLongitudeStart', 'year', 'startDayOfYear']\n",
    "\n",
    "stations_df = all_merged[station_key].copy()\n",
    "stations_df['decimalLatitudeStart'] = pd.to_numeric(stations_df['decimalLatitudeStart'], errors='coerce').round(5)\n",
    "stations_df['decimalLongitudeStart'] = pd.to_numeric(stations_df['decimalLongitudeStart'], errors='coerce').round(5)\n",
    "stations_df['year'] = pd.to_numeric(stations_df['year'], errors='coerce').round().astype('Int64')\n",
    "stations_df['startDayOfYear'] = pd.to_numeric(stations_df['startDayOfYear'], errors='coerce').round().astype('Int64')\n",
    "stations_df = stations_df.dropna(subset=station_cols_required)\n",
    "\n",
    "stations = stations_df.drop_duplicates(station_key).shape[0]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 13,
   "id": "c6b5eccc",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "excel_path_rank = 'Final/merged_all_final.parquet'\n",
    "df_rank = pd.read_parquet(excel_path_rank)\n",
    "\n",
    "rank_series_clean = df_rank['taxonRank'].astype(str).str.strip()\n",
    "total_records_rank = len(rank_series_clean)\n",
    "\n",
    "rank_counts = rank_series_clean.value_counts(dropna=False)\n",
    "rank_percent = (rank_counts / total_records_rank) * 100.0 if total_records_rank > 0 else rank_counts\n",
    "\n",
    "rank_summary_df = (\n",
    "    rank_percent.rename('PercentOfRecords')\n",
    "    .reset_index()\n",
    "    .rename(columns={'index': 'taxonRank'})\n",
    "    .sort_values('PercentOfRecords', ascending=False)\n",
    "    .reset_index(drop=True)\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "rank_percent_map = rank_summary_df.set_index('taxonRank')['PercentOfRecords'].to_dict()\n",
    "\n",
    "species_pct = rank_percent_map.get('Species')\n",
    "genus_pct = rank_percent_map.get('Genus')\n",
    "family_pct = rank_percent_map.get('Family')\n",
    "order_pct = rank_percent_map.get('Order')\n",
    "class_pct = rank_percent_map.get('Class')\n",
    "phylum_pct = rank_percent_map.get('Phylum')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 16,
   "id": "ecdd579d-8ee0-4854-a650-9c2bb518c8b3",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/markdown": [
       "The records in BENTH-MARC represent two and a half centuries of sampling, spanning from 1773 to 2026. In total, there are 223,349 stations with 3,760,265 georeferenced taxon records of 11,892 taxa (of those 8,442 at species level) from 826 datasets. Stations are defined as unique combinations of dataset name, coordinates and year. Each record is the observation of a certain taxon at a specific location and time, and can have associated information about the number of individuals per area and/or their biomass. The total number of taxa is here equivalent to the total number of unique AphiaIDs in the database, although some AphiaIDs represent other taxonomic levels than species. As the same taxon may have been identified at different taxonomic resolutions at different stations, the total number of taxa is an overestimation of the total number of species. @fig-tax_resolution shows the taxonomic resolution of the records. The majority of the records (71%) were identified to species level, followed by genus (15%) and family level (4%)."
      ],
      "text/plain": [
       "<IPython.core.display.Markdown object>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "display(Markdown(\n",
    "    f\"The records in BENTH-MARC represent two and a half centuries of sampling, spanning from {start_year:.0f} to {end_year:.0f}. \"\n",
    "    f\"In total, there are {stations:,.0f} stations with {records:,.0f} georeferenced taxon records of {taxa:,.0f} taxa (of those {species_taxa:,.0f} at species level) from {datasets} datasets. \"\n",
    "    f\"Stations are defined as unique combinations of dataset name, coordinates and year. \"\n",
    "    f\"Each record is the observation of a certain taxon at a specific location and time, and can have associated information about the number of individuals per area and/or their biomass. \"\n",
    "    f\"The total number of taxa is here equivalent to the total number of unique AphiaIDs in the database, although some AphiaIDs represent other taxonomic levels than species. \"\n",
    "    f\"As the same taxon may have been identified at different taxonomic resolutions at different stations, the total number of taxa is an overestimation of the total number of species. \"\n",
    "     f\"@fig-tax_resolution shows the taxonomic resolution of the records. The majority of the records ({species_pct:.0f}%) were identified to species level, followed by genus ({genus_pct:.0f}%) and family level ({family_pct:.0f}%).\"\n",
    "   ))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 21,
   "id": "731e27ac",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1000x400 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "#| label: fig-tax_resolution\n",
    "#| fig-cap: \"Taxonomic resolution of all records of benthic invertebrates in the BENTH-MARC data collection. The bars show records per rank from phylum to species.\"\n",
    "#| fig-pos: \"H\"\n",
    "\n",
    "# Load data\n",
    "merged_path = 'Final/merged_all_final.parquet'\n",
    "df_merged = pd.read_parquet(merged_path)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Ensure required column exists\n",
    "if 'taxonRank' not in df_merged.columns:\n",
    "    raise KeyError(\"Column 'taxonRank' not found in Final/merged_all_final.parquet\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Normalize rank values for consistent matching and display labels\n",
    "rank_series = df_merged['taxonRank'].fillna('').astype(str).str.strip().str.lower().str.capitalize()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Keep the exact order requested (title case for display)\n",
    "target_order = ['Phylum', 'Class', 'Order', 'Family', 'Genus', 'Species', 'Subspecies']\n",
    "\n",
    "# Count records per requested rank\n",
    "rank_counts = rank_series.value_counts(dropna=False)\n",
    "target_counts = rank_counts.reindex(target_order, fill_value=0).astype(int)\n",
    "\n",
    "# DataFrame for plotting\n",
    "rank_counts_df = target_counts.rename_axis('Rank').reset_index(name='Count')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Plot\n",
    "color = '#9DC8D2'\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 4))\n",
    "plt.bar(rank_counts_df['Rank'], rank_counts_df['Count'], color=color)\n",
    "plt.ylabel('Number of records', fontsize=10)\n",
    "plt.xticks(rotation=0, fontsize=10)\n",
    "plt.gca().yaxis.set_major_formatter(plt.matplotlib.ticker.StrMethodFormatter('{x:,.0f}'))\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.savefig(\"Figures/taxonomic_levels.png\", dpi=300, bbox_inches=\"tight\", facecolor=\"white\")\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
