   "source": [
    "#| include: false\n",
    "\n",
    "# Load the dataset\n",
    "try:\n",
    "    df_geo\n",
    "except NameError:\n",
    "    df_geo = pd.read_parquet('Final/merged_all_final.parquet')  # Load if not already in memory\n",
    "# Pick the taxa column\n",
    "taxon_col = 'acceptedScientificName'\n",
    "if taxon_col not in df_geo.columns:\n",
    "    # Find potential candidates for the taxon column\n",
    "    candidates = [c for c in df_geo.columns if 'ScientificName' in c]\n",
    "    taxon_col = candidates[0] if len(candidates) > 0 else df_geo.columns[-1]\n",
    "# Clean category text (same robust logic as Cell 55)\n",
    "df_geo['geomorphologicalCategory'] = df_geo['geomorphologicalCategory'].fillna('Outside geomorphological categories')\n",
    "df_geo['geomorphologicalCategory'] = df_geo['geomorphologicalCategory'].replace('', 'Outside geomorphological categories')\n",
    "df_geo['geomorphologicalCategory'] = df_geo['geomorphologicalCategory'].astype(str).str.strip()\n",
    "df_geo.loc[df_geo['geomorphologicalCategory'].isin(['nan', 'None']), 'geomorphologicalCategory'] = 'Outside geomorphological categories'\n",
    "# Aggregate data\n",
    "grp = df_geo.groupby('geomorphologicalCategory', dropna=False)\n",
    "summary_table = grp.agg(\n",
    "    records=('geomorphologicalCategory', 'size'),\n",
    "    unique_taxa=(taxon_col, pd.Series.nunique)\n",
    ").reset_index()\n",
    "# Compute proportions and ratios\n",
    "summary_table['prop_records'] = summary_table['records'] / summary_table['records'].sum()\n",
    "summary_table['prop_unique_taxa'] = summary_table['unique_taxa'] / summary_table['unique_taxa'].sum()\n",
    "summary_table['taxa_to_records_ratio'] = summary_table['prop_unique_taxa'] / summary_table['prop_records']\n",
    "# Limit decimals for numeric columns\n",
    "summary_table['prop_records'] = summary_table['prop_records'].round(2)\n",
    "summary_table['prop_unique_taxa'] = summary_table['prop_unique_taxa'].round(2)\n",
    "summary_table['taxa_to_records_ratio'] = summary_table['taxa_to_records_ratio'].round(2)\n",
    "# Rename columns with row breaks for better formatting\n",
    "summary_table = summary_table.rename(columns={\n",
    "    'geomorphologicalCategory': 'Geomorphological\\ncategory',\n",
    "    'records': 'Number of\\nrecords',\n",
    "    'unique_taxa': 'Unique\\ntaxa',\n",
    "    'prop_records': 'Proportion of\\nall records',\n",
    "    'prop_unique_taxa': 'Proportion of\\nall taxa',\n",
    "    'taxa_to_records_ratio': 'Taxa-to-records\\nratio'\n",
    "})\n",
    "# Add row breaks to category names for better formatting\n",
    "summary_table['Geomorphological\\ncategory'] = summary_table['Geomorphological\\ncategory'].replace({\n",
    "    'Shallow shelf': 'Shallow\\nshelf',\n",
    "    'Medium shelf': 'Medium\\nshelf',\n",
    "    'Deep shelf': 'Deep\\nshelf',\n",
    "    'Abyssal plains and hadal': 'Abyssal\\nplains\\nand\\nhadal',\n",
    "    'Abyssal plains and hadal zones': 'Abyssal\\nplains\\nand hadal',\n",
    "    'Abyssal mountains and hills': 'Abyssal\\nmountains\\nand hills',\n",
    "    'Outside geomorphological categories': 'Outside\\ngeomorphological\\ncategories'\n",
    "})\n",
    "# Sort the table by proportion of records\n",
    "summary_table = summary_table.sort_values('Taxa-to-records\\nratio', ascending=True)\n",
    "# Create a styled table as a plot\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(12, 6))  # Adjust figure size to fit the table\n",
    "ax.axis('tight')\n",
    "ax.axis('off')\n",
    "# Create the table\n",
    "table = ax.table(\n",
    "    cellText=summary_table.values,\n",
    "    colLabels=summary_table.columns,\n",
    "    cellLoc='center',\n",
    "    loc='center'\n",
    ")\n",
    "# Style the table\n",
    "table.auto_set_font_size(False)\n",
    "table.set_fontsize(10)\n",
    "# Adjust column widths and row heights\n",
    "for key, cell in table.get_celld().items():\n",
    "    cell.set_text_props(ha='center', va='center')  # Center-align text\n",
    "    if key[0] == 0:  # Header row\n",
    "        cell.set_fontsize(10)\n",
    "        cell.set_text_props(weight='bold')  # Bold header text\n",
    "        cell.set_facecolor('#9DC8D2')  # Set header background color\n",
    "        cell.set_height(0.15)  # Adjust header row height\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        cell.set_height(0.12)  # Adjust row height for data rows\n",
    "        if key[1] == 0:  # Geomorphological category column\n",
    "            cell.set_text_props(weight='bold')  # Make category names bold\n",
    "# Show the table\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2d86a632-4885-40f2-945a-ecb614a8c766",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Similarly, sampling effort and the availability of records varies considerably over geomorphological features on a pan-Arctic scale. In @fig-geo, the global geomorphological features mapped by Harris *et al.* [@harris2014] have been merged into a coarser set of categories to visualize the distribution of sampling effort in terms of number of records as well as the number of taxa per category. The deep and medium shelves are considerably more well-sampled than other areas (@fig-geo), reflecting the high sampling effort in Barents Sea and the Norwegian Sea that could also be seen in @fig-lme. The Norwegian Sea and Barents Sea are bordered by innumerous fjords and the Barents Sea has numerous troughs that are deep enough to be categorized as medium and deep shelf. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 49,
   "id": "b7a22511-81d9-4fc3-b9f1-d12d10bc4f4e",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/markdown": [
       "The shelves are most easily accessible and the deep, medium and shallow shelf have together received a staggering 86% of the overall sampling effort in terms of number of records sampled. At the other end of the spectrum, the hadal zones together with abyssal plains, mountains and hills have collectively only received 2% of the total sampling effort. Due to near-shore locations that fall outside the coverage of the shapefiles provided by Harris *et al.* [@harris2014], 5% of the records are categorized as Outside geomorphological categories."
      ],
      "text/plain": [
       "<IPython.core.display.Markdown object>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "display(Markdown(\n",
    "    f\"The shelves are most easily accessible and the deep, medium and shallow shelf have together received a staggering {formatted_percentage_shelf_pct}% of the overall sampling effort in terms of number of records sampled. \"\n",
    "    f\"At the other end of the spectrum, the hadal zones together with abyssal plains, mountains and hills have collectively only received {formatted_percentage_abyssal_pct}% of the total sampling effort. Due to near-shore \" \n",
    "    f\"locations that fall outside the coverage of the shapefiles provided by Harris *et al.* [@harris2014], {formatted_percentage_outside_pct}% of the records are \" \n",
    "    f\"categorized as Outside geomorphological categories.\"\n",
    "))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 52,
   "id": "97ab9495",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/markdown": [
       "The proportional relationship between the number of records and the number of taxa (i.e. the taxa-to-records ratio) changes with sampling pressure and depth. Wherever the ratio is one to one, the category contributes taxa in line with its share of records. At lower ratios, the category contributes fewer unique taxa than would be expected. The lowest taxa-to-records ratio is found at the deep shelf (0.7), and the ratio generally gradually increases as the number of records decreases and the depth increases, with abyssal plains and hadal zones having the highest ratio at 10.8. There are a few exceptions to the depth-related pattern, such as the shallow and medium shelf having fewer records and a higher ratio than the deep shelf, and ridges having fewer records and a lower ratio than the abyssal mountains and hills. The deeper areas are generally less well-sampled and each sample has few individuals, meaning that each sample from these areas has a higher probability of encountering taxa that had not been previously recorded in the area. The ratios indicate that the low numbers of unique taxa in the deep areas are a consequence of low sampling effort rather than a true representation of the actual biodiversity in these areas, and that the deep areas are likely to be considerably more taxon-rich than current data suggest. "
      ],
      "text/plain": [
       "<IPython.core.display.Markdown object>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "display(Markdown(\n",
    "    f\"The proportional relationship between the number of records and the number of taxa (i.e. the taxa-to-records ratio) changes with sampling pressure and depth. Wherever the ratio is one to one, the category contributes taxa \" \n",
    "    f\"in line with its share of records. At lower ratios, the category contributes fewer unique taxa than would be expected. The lowest taxa-to-records ratio is found at the deep shelf ({deep_shelf_ratio:.1f}), \" \n",
    "    f\"and the ratio generally gradually increases as the number of records decreases and the depth increases, \" \n",
    "    f\"with abyssal plains and hadal zones having the highest ratio at {abyssal_plains_hadal_ratio:.1f}. There are a few exceptions to the depth-related pattern, \" \n",
    "    f\"such as the shallow and medium shelf having fewer records and a higher ratio than the deep shelf, and ridges having fewer records and a lower ratio than the abyssal mountains and hills. \"\n",
    "    f\"The deeper areas are generally less well-sampled and each sample has few individuals, meaning that each sample from these areas has a higher probability of encountering taxa that had not been previously recorded in the area. \" \n",
    "    f\"The ratios indicate that the low numbers of unique taxa in the deep areas are a consequence of low sampling effort rather than a true representation \" \n",
    "    f\"of the actual biodiversity in these areas, and that the deep areas are likely to be considerably more taxon-rich than current data suggest. \"\n",
    "))"
